llvm-3.7.1 forward port: the loop-closed phis are removed after the late LICM

This patch CHANGES THE -O2 PIPELINE: one more pass, the instruction
simplifier, after the loop-invariant code motion that follows loop
unrolling. It is the whole code change of upstream r271602 (a485984c0c88,
2016-06-02, D20606, "[PM] Schedule InstSimplify after late LICM run, to
clean up LCSSA nodes", first released in 3.9.0; its test left out): "LCSSA
is implicitly run as a pass dependency of LICM. However no cleanup pass was
run after this, so the LCSSA nodes ended in the optimized output." The
upstream date is later than a 3.7 compiler, so the base's side is read from
the base, not from the history.

What the base shows. A value a loop leaves behind reaches code generation
either through a one-entry phi in the exit block (3.7.1 and 3.8.1 as
released) or directly (3.9.0 on), and loop strength reduction serves a use
after the loop only in the second case:
- 0xcb57e0 (a table of name and function pairs searched by name, the
  function called after the loop): the base bases the walking register on
  the function field (`add r13, 8` before the loop, `mov rsi, [r13 - 8]`
  in it, `mov rax, [r13]` after); with the phi the register is based on
  the entry (`mov rsi, [r13]`, `mov rax, [r13 + 8]`). Six source forms
  (indexed, a reference to the entry, begin and end pointers, search then
  use, locals) do not reach the base's bytes with the phi (round 276B).
- 0xf1a950 and its thirteen copies (a destructor that finds an entry and
  removes it): the base carries the byte offset through find()'s loop
  (`add rbx, 0x10` beside `inc eax`) and uses it after; with the phi the
  offset is recomputed after the loop and one more register is saved.
The cause was found on 0xcb57e0 with the prebuilt releases (round 276B,
b276/fam/): 3.9.1's llc over 3.7.1's optimised bitcode gives 3.7.1's code,
so the difference is in the IR; `opt -O2 -debug-pass=Structure` has "Remove
redundant instructions" after the last LICM from 3.9.1 on and not in 3.7.1
or 3.8.1; the prebuilt 3.7.1's `opt -instsimplify` over its own optimised
bitcode, then its llc, gives the base's three instructions.

Swept alone over the game module built by the installed 3.7.1 with its
eight patches (round 276B, 5,497 rows, 6,659 functions): 40 functions
change; 15 rows become equal (5,476 to 5,491: 0xcb57e0 and the fourteen of
the 0xf1a950 family) and none is lost.

What stands against (drafts, not rows): 0x1af9d0 (a search loop returning
the entry found) is equal WITH the phi (the base rebuilds the entry from the
index after the loop: `shl rsi, 4; add r8, rsi`) and 1 of 22 without it;
five source forms do not bring it back, and the prebuilt 3.9.1 does not
give it either. 0x583bb0 is equal with the phi and 30 of 45 without (the
state it has under 4.0.0, where it wants its reads reordered). 0xdacb80
moves the other way, 43 to 76 of 78. So the base's rule may be narrower
than upstream's pass; no upstream state between the two was found.

--- a/lib/Transforms/IPO/PassManagerBuilder.cpp
+++ b/lib/Transforms/IPO/PassManagerBuilder.cpp
@@ -389,6 +389,9 @@
     // outer loop. LICM pass can help to promote the runtime check out if the
     // checked value is loop invariant.
     MPM.add(createLICMPass());
+
+    // Get rid of LCSSA nodes.
+    MPM.add(createInstructionSimplifierPass());
   }
 
   // After vectorization and unrolling, assume intrinsics may tell us more
